Z||000000||corbz||09-02-99||10:58 AM||||Im looking to buy my first Fiero (once I sell my current car) I want a GT with a fastback. I want opinions on the best year to get if there is any difference. Thanks in advance for you input.
Z||000001||Patrick||09-02-99||11:24 AM||||<br>There's no doubt that the "best" year is the '88 simply due to the suspension improvements. I'll be surprised if anyone disagrees.<p>
Z||000002||terryk||09-02-99||01:48 PM||||Yes, the 88. The only downside is that parts are far more expensive since it was in production for only one year. Mainly suspension.  
Z||000003||lowCG||09-02-99||02:14 PM||||Yeah,the '88 is the most advanced version offered by GM,with it's improved suspension,and brakes.<br>I would look for the car with the options you really want,and with the least miles best condition,etc.regardless of year,as it's harder to restore these cars the older they get,and I'm happy with the older suspension,provided it's properly modified,but that's just me.1984 models are the ones I probably will never own.
Z||000004||batboy||09-02-99||04:31 PM||||The 1988 Fiero GT is very desirable for a number of reasons mentioned above, thus are getting over-priced because of their popularity and scarcity (under 7,000 GTs made). There's nothing wrong with the 86 or 87 GTs. You'll probably find a better deal and can use the money you saved to upgrade your suspension and brakes and maybe enough cash left to install a nice stereo too.
Z||000005||toopak||09-03-99||09:34 AM||||I have an 88 GT that bought from orig. owner back in 95 with 50k miles. Orig. wanted to inst V-8 but too much cutting involved. DON'T get a FIERO unless you intend to work on it yourself.  Have had the cradle/engine/trans out of mine twice and not too bad once you do it 1st time. Try to get an original unspoiled car in decent shape but dont be surprised if you have to do work on it .
